WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.250
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar. I received this Mathcounts problem by email from Bill.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:12.120
Find all positive integers n for which n squared plus 45 is a perfect square.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:19.529
Can you figure it out? Give this problem a try and when you're ready to keep watching the video for the solution.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:29.670
Suppose n squared plus 45 equals a perfect square: x squared.

00:00:31.029 --> 00:00:37.409
We now subtract n squared from both sides of the equation to get 45 equals x squared minus n squared.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:40.710
We now can perform a little trick.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:48.780
We have the difference of two squares and we can factor that as the product of x plus n and x minus n.

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:53.878
So we're looking for two factors of 45.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:03.390
The possibilities for this can be 45 times 1, 15 times 3, and 9 times 5.

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:08.760
We now need to set the equations equal to these factors.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:17.309
Since x plus n is larger than x minus n, we will set x plus n equal to the larger factor.

00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:20.699
This is because x and n are positive integers.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:28.349
So from the first possibility we set x plus n to equal 45 and x minus n to equal 1.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:33.240
In the next possibility way of x plus n equals 15 and x minus n equals 3

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.500
We then have x plus n equals 9 and x minus n equals 5.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.920
We can solve each of these equations for x and n.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.760
In the first set of equations, for example,

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.540
we can add up the two equations to get that 2x is equal to 46,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.360
and then we divide by 2 to get x is equal to 23.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.509
We can then go back and solve that n is equal to 22.

00:01:55.119 --> 00:01:57.869
We'll use the same trick for the next two equations as well.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:04.829
We end up with x is equal to 9 and n equals 6 and then x is equal to 7 and n equals 2.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:10.420
So you know three possibilities n can equal 22, 6 &amp; 2.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:16.920
And those are the only ways in positive integers that n squared plus 45 will be equal to a perfect square.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.660
Did you figure it out?

